  "pageGuide": "How to use this page",
  "guideReloadTitle": "Reload the app",
  "guideReloadSummary": "Reload the inspected app from any DevConnect page, even while typing: Flutter rebuilds its widgets, React Native reloads from Metro, Android recreates the current activity.",
  "guideReloadSteps": "[⌘ R] Reload the selected app, or every connected app when none is selected\n[⇧ ⌘ R] Hot restart: runs a Flutter app's onHotRestartRequest, otherwise reloads like ⌘ R\nOr click the reload buttons in the bottom bar — their tooltips show the shortcut",
  "guideReloadTip": "Flutter's ⌘ R rebuilds the widget tree and keeps state, but doesn't load code edits — use your IDE's hot reload for that.",
  "guideJsonSearchTitle": "Search inside a body",
  "guideJsonSearchSummary": "Tree, JSON and the code view (TypeScript, Dart or Kotlin) each have their own search: it ignores case, highlights every match and scrolls to the current one.",
  "guideJsonSearchSteps": "[⌘ F] Jump to the view's search box — click inside the body first\n[↵] Go to the next match\n[⇧ ↵] Go to the previous match\n[Esc] Clear the search",
  "guideJsonSearchTip": "Tree search unfolds nodes and loads later pages to show up to 500 matches; JSON counts up to 100,000.",
  "guideLayoutZoomTitle": "Zoom a layout capture",
  "guideLayoutZoomSummary": "Zoom and frame a capture on the Layout page from the keyboard — the keys work as soon as a capture is shown.",
  "guideLayoutZoomSteps": "[⌘ +] Zoom in, 1.25× per press\n[⌘ −] Zoom out\n[⌘ 0] Actual size: 100% is the app's own screen size\n[⌘ 1] Fit the whole screen in the panel\n[⌘ 2] Zoom to the selected element",
  "guideLayoutZoomTip": "Click the zoom percentage for 50, 100, 200 or 400%; pinch to zoom and drag to pan.",
  "guideLayoutMeasureTitle": "Measure spacing",
  "guideLayoutMeasureSummary": "Hold Option to see the distances between the selected element and the one under the pointer, as design tools do.",
  "guideLayoutMeasureSteps": "Select an element: click it on the capture or pick it in the tree\n[⌥] Hold Option and point at another element\nRead the gaps between separate elements, or the insets when one holds the other\nRelease Option to see the hovered element's box model again",
  "guideLayoutMeasureTip": "Overlapping elements show no distance — measuring needs one element inside the other, or a gap between them.",
  "guideLayoutTreeTitle": "Walk the element tree",
  "guideLayoutTreeSummary": "Move through the captured element tree with the arrow keys once it has focus — click a row or an element on the capture.",
  "guideLayoutTreeSteps": "[↑ ↓] Move to the row above or below\n[→] Open the row; press again to step into its first child\n[←] Close the row; press again to step out to its parent\n[Esc] Clear the selection",
  "guideLayoutTreeTip": "Type in \"Find by name or value\" to search names, props and state; ↵ and ⇧ ↵ step through the matches.",
  "guideAndroidEmulatorTitle": "Android emulator",
  "guideAndroidEmulatorSummary": "Apps in the Android emulator reach this Mac at 10.0.2.2 (Genymotion: 10.0.3.2), so there's nothing to set up.",
  "guideAndroidEmulatorSteps": "Check that Settings › \"Connection\" › \"Server\" shows \"Server Running\"\nNative Android: pass enabled = BuildConfig.DEBUG to installForApp — it's off by default\nRun a debug build in the emulator; adb reverse isn't needed\nFind the app in the device bar at the bottom of the window",
  "guideAndroidEmulatorTip": "DevConnect starts on port 9090 at every launch; a port changed under \"Server\" lasts until you quit, and the SDK's port must match.",
  "guideAndroidUsbTitle": "Android phone over USB",
  "guideAndroidUsbSummary": "adb reverse points the phone's localhost at this Mac over the cable, so the app connects without Wi‑Fi.",
  "guideAndroidUsbSteps": "Turn on USB debugging, plug in the phone and allow this Mac when asked\nPress \"Run ADB Reverse\" in Settings › \"Connection\" › \"USB Connection\", or run the command below\nStart the app — restart a native Android app that was already running\nNot connecting? Press \"Devices\" there to check that adb sees the phone",
  "guideAndroidUsbTip": "Run it again after replugging, restarting adb or rebooting; with several devices plugged in, run adb -s with the phone's serial in Terminal.",
  "guideIosSimulatorTitle": "iOS simulator",
  "guideIosSimulatorSummary": "The simulator shares the Mac's network, so a debug build reaches DevConnect at localhost with no setup.",
  "guideIosSimulatorSteps": "Check that Settings › \"Connection\" › \"Server\" shows \"Server Running\"\nRun a debug build of the app in the simulator\nFind it in the device bar at the bottom of the window",
  "guideIosSimulatorTip": "Start order doesn't matter: React Native and Flutter apps keep retrying, so they join a few seconds after DevConnect starts.",
  "guideIphoneTitle": "iPhone",
  "guideIphoneSummary": "On the same Wi‑Fi as the Mac, a debug build usually finds DevConnect by itself; if it doesn't, set the Mac's address as host.",
  "guideIphoneSteps": "Put the iPhone and the Mac on the same Wi‑Fi — guest networks often block this\nRun the debug build and allow local network access when iOS asks\nWait a few seconds: React Native finds the Mac through Metro, Flutter through a broadcast\nNot listed? Copy the Mac's address from Settings › \"Connection\" › \"Network\"\nSet it as host in the SDK's init (code below), then restart the app",
  "guideIphoneTip": "A USB cable alone can't connect an iPhone app — the phone has to reach the Mac over the network.",
  "guideTroubleshootTitle": "App won't connect",
  "guideTroubleshootSummary": "Most connection problems come down to a few causes — check them in this order.",
  "guideTroubleshootSteps": "Settings › \"Connection\" › \"Server\" shows \"Server Running\" and no port error\nThe app is a debug build; native Android also needs enabled = BuildConfig.DEBUG\nThe SDK's port matches the server's — both start at 9090\nPhones share the Mac's Wi‑Fi, or Android's port is forwarded with adb reverse\nOn Wi‑Fi, macOS lets DevConnect accept incoming connections (System Settings › Network › Firewall)",
  "guideTroubleshootTip": "Still stuck? Set host to an address from Settings › \"Connection\" › \"Network\" and pass auto: false (Flutter: auto_: false) so reconnects keep it.",
  "guideSdkReactNativeSummary": "Install the package and call init once at startup — fetch and XHR (so axios too), console logs, errors and performance are captured from then on.",
  "guideSdkReactNativeSteps": "Add devconnect-manage-kit with yarn or npm (commands below)\nCall DevConnect.init once at startup, before the first request\nFor state, add devConnectReduxMiddleware (Redux), devConnectMiddleware (Zustand) or setupMobxSpy (MobX)\nRun a debug build — init does nothing in release unless you pass enabled: true",
  "guideSdkReactNativeTip": "init runs once per app start, so after changing host or port do a full reload — Fast Refresh keeps the old settings.",
  "guideSdkFlutterSummary": "Start the app through initAndRunApp: dart:io HTTP (http, Dio, Chopper…), print and debugPrint, errors and performance are captured with no extra code.",
  "guideSdkFlutterSteps": "Run flutter pub add devconnect_manage_kit\nStart the app with DevConnect.initAndRunApp in main(), passing an async runApp callback\nFor Riverpod or BLoC, call DevConnect.reportStateChange or reportBlocChange from your observer\nRun a debug build — profile and release builds stay off",
  "guideSdkFlutterTip": "Put Firebase and other startup code inside the runApp callback — calling ensureInitialized() before initAndRunApp causes a zone mismatch.",
  "guideSdkAndroidSummary": "Add the Maven Central dependency and call installForApp in Application.onCreate: println output, crashes, ANRs, performance and ViewModel state are captured; HTTP needs the OkHttp interceptor.",
  "guideSdkAndroidSteps": "Add io.github.buivietphi:devconnect-android:1.2.0 to app/build.gradle.kts\nCall DevConnect.installForApp in Application.onCreate with enabled = BuildConfig.DEBUG — it's off by default\nAdd DevConnect.okHttpInterceptor() to every OkHttpClient, Retrofit's included\nLog with DCLog instead of android.util.Log; for Timber, plant a Tree that calls DevConnectTimberHelper.log\nMake sure the app's manifest requests the INTERNET permission",
  "guideSdkAndroidTip": "The SDK looks for DevConnect once, at launch — start DevConnect (and adb reverse) first, or restart the app.",
  "guideMockApiTitle": "Mock an API",
  "guideMockApiSummary": "Answer matching requests with your own status, headers and body, optionally delayed, without touching the server or the app.",
  "guideMockApiSteps": "Select a device in the bottom bar, open \"Mock Server\" and press \"Add rule\"\nSet \"Method\", \"Status\", \"URL pattern\" (a regex matched anywhere in the URL) and the body\nPress \"Save changes\", then \"Push all\" — it sends the whole rule list, and later saves reach the device on their own\nCheck Network › \"Mock Audit\" for the requests a rule answered\nUse a rule's switch to pause it, or \"Clear device\" to remove every rule",
  "guideMockApiTip": "Android needs DevConnect.mockServerInterceptor() on OkHttp; React Native mocks fetch calls only, not axios or XMLHttpRequest.",
  "guideStateControlsTitle": "Control state from the desktop",
  "guideStateControlsSummary": "The State Inspector's Controls tab watches state paths, saves snapshots to send back, and dispatches actions into the app.",
  "guideStateControlsSteps": "Pick one device in the bottom bar, open State Inspector and switch to \"Controls\"\nSubscriptions: pin a path such as auth.user.name; works on every platform with no app code\nSnapshots: \"Take snapshot\" saves the latest state; \"Restore\" sends it back once the app handles onStateRestore\nDispatch action: type a JSON object with a \"type\" such as \"LOGOUT\" and press \"Dispatch\"; React Native needs connectReduxStore(store), Flutter and Android need onReduxDispatch\nChanges they cause show in \"Timeline\" when the app's state library reports them",
  "guideStateControlsTip": "Subscriptions read the latest reported state: the whole store with Redux, but only the store or provider that changed last with Zustand, MobX, Riverpod or a ViewModel. If Restore or Dispatch does nothing, the app hasn't set its handler; a handler that throws shows in the Console.",
  "guideNetworkConditionsTitle": "Slow or offline network",
  "guideNetworkConditionsSummary": "Delay the apps' HTTP requests or make them fail, without touching device settings — DevConnect's own connection keeps working.",
  "guideNetworkConditionsSteps": "In Network, open the gauge menu \"Network conditions\" in the toolbar\nPick \"Slow (+500 ms)\" or \"Slow 3G (~2 s)\" to delay every request\nPick \"Offline\" to make requests fail at once, like a dead network\nPick \"Normal\" when you're done — the icon stays highlighted until then\nOn Android only OkHttp traffic slows down, through DevConnect.okHttpInterceptor()",
  "guideNetworkConditionsTip": "It applies to every connected app, and to apps that connect later, until you pick \"Normal\".",
  "guideReplayTitle": "Replay a request",
  "guideReplaySummary": "Have the app send a captured request again — same method, URL, headers and body — without tapping through its screens.",
  "guideReplaySteps": "In Network, right-click a request and choose \"Replay request\"\nOr open the request and click the repeat icon in its header\nThe replay shows up as a new row in the list",
  "guideReplayTip": "Android reruns recent OkHttp calls through the app's own client and interceptors; Flutter and React Native resend the captured headers as they are.",
  "guideHarExportTitle": "Export to HAR",
  "guideHarExportSummary": "Save the requests Network lists as a standard HAR 1.2 file — headers, bodies, status and timing included — to open in other tools.",
  "guideHarExportSteps": "Narrow the \"HTTP\" list with the device, method, status and URL filters\nClick the download icon \"Export the listed requests as HAR\" in the toolbar\nChoose where to save the .har file\nOpen it in Chrome DevTools or Charles, or share it with your team",
  "guideHarExportTip": "Only what the HTTP list shows is saved, so your filters decide what's in the file; \"Protocols\" entries aren't included.",
  "guideDatabaseQueryTitle": "Query the app's database",
  "guideDatabaseQuerySummary": "Browse the tables of a SQLite database inside the app and run SQL on it, right from DevConnect.",
  "guideDatabaseQuerySteps": "Register the database in the app with DevConnect.registerDatabase (snippets below)\nOpen Database and click a table to preview its first 50 rows\nEdit the SQL and press \"Run\" — results stop at 1,000 rows\nClick \"Reload tables\" after the app changes its tables",
  "guideDatabaseQueryTip": "Queries run on the database of the last table you clicked, otherwise on the first one registered.",
  "guideBigJsonTitle": "Large bodies",
  "guideBigJsonSummary": "Big bodies open without freezing DevConnect: they're parsed in the background, and Tree and JSON build only what's on screen.",
  "guideBigJsonSteps": "Click \"Show … more\" under a long Tree list to load the next 200 items\nUse \"Copy value\" on long Tree strings — Tree shows their first 2,000 characters\nSwitch to JSON to scroll and search the whole document\nUse \"Copy JSON\" or \"Save as file\" there — both take the full body\nJSON lines over 20,000 characters show only their start; copy or save to get all of it",
  "guideBigJsonTip": "When the body is a list of objects, Tree offers \"Table\": it shows 500 rows, but \"Download table as CSV\" saves every matching row.",
  "guideCustomCommandsTitle": "Custom commands",
  "guideCustomCommandsSummary": "Run code inside the app from DevConnect — clear a cache, log out, seed test data — and see what it returns.",
  "guideCustomCommandsSteps": "Register a handler in the app with DevConnect.registerCommand (snippets below)\nOpen \"Custom commands\" and click a command the app listed, or type its name\nAdd arguments as a JSON object if the command needs any\nPress \"Run\", or ↵ in the \"Command\" field, and read the result below",
  "guideCustomCommandsTip": "Flutter and React Native handlers can be async; a thrown error shows as the result, and no answer times out after 10 seconds.",
